tel 1.0

base inimitable
import soundness

info   UUIDs as well-formed values: generated, written as checked literals, parsed with typed errors
detail
    _Inimitable_ represents a [UUID](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Universally_unique_identifier),
    a 128-bit identifier unique enough to assign without coordination, as a `Uuid` value which
    is known to be well-formed. A string can hold a malformed identifier until something
    parses it and fails; a `Uuid` cannot. A literal written with `uuid"…"` is checked as the
    code compiles, text parsed at runtime raises a typed `Uuid.Error`, and the identifier's
    two 64-bit halves and sixteen bytes are available directly.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely

    val fresh: Uuid = Uuid()
    val known: Uuid = uuid"a0cb16f0-d41e-4c28-862f-bd6164bbcc8c"
    val parsed: Uuid = Uuid.parse(t"a0cb16f0-d41e-4c28-862f-bd6164bbcc8c")

    known.text      // t"a0cb16f0-d41e-4c28-862f-bd6164bbcc8c"
    known.in[Data]  // the sixteen bytes
    fresh ^ known   // the bitwise exclusive-or of the two
    ```

    A `Uuid` shows, encodes and decodes as its canonical hyphenated text through givens in
    its companion, so `as[Uuid]` reads one from text without any import. For a consumer which
    is generic over the `Uuids` domain (`[uuid: Instantiable across Uuids from Text]`), the
    instance making `Uuid` that domain's representative is
    `uuidInterfaces.soundnessUuid`, a given which must be imported by name, since `import
    uuidInterfaces.*` does not import givens; it is declared in the `anticipation` package.

    The library has a single source set, `inimitable.core`, for every platform. Everything
    is exported to the `soundness` umbrella.

refer inimitable.Uuid
refer inimitable.uuid

entry .Uuid   a 128-bit universally unique identifier, as its two 64-bit halves
  detail
      A `Uuid` is a case class of its most and least significant 64 bits, `#msb` and
      `#lsb`. It is made fresh with `Uuid()`, which draws a random (version 4) identifier
      from `java.util.UUID.randomUUID`; written literally with `uuid"…"`; parsed from text
      with `.parse`, which raises `Uuid.Error`, or with `.extract`, which gives an
      `Optional`; or built directly from its two halves.

      The companion is also an extractor, so a pattern matches text which is a valid UUID:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      t"not-a-uuid" match
        case Uuid(id) => id
        case _        => Uuid()
      ```

      A `Uuid` renders as its canonical lowercase hyphenated form through `#text`, `show`,
      `encode`, `inspect` and as a message; `uuid.in[Data]` gives its sixteen bytes, most
      significant half first, each half big-endian. `#^` and `#unary_~` treat it as the bit
      pattern it is, for deriving one identifier from others.
  refer inimitable.uuid
  refer inimitable.jvmInstanceId
  entry .apply   a fresh random UUID
      Draws a version 4 identifier from `java.util.UUID.randomUUID`. Each call gives a new
      one; `jvmInstanceId` is one drawn once per running program. The case-class
      constructor `Uuid(msb, lsb)` builds one from its two halves.
  entry .parse   reads a UUID from its text, raising `Uuid.Error` if the text is not one
      Accepts the forms `java.util.UUID.fromString` does: five groups of hexadecimal digits
      separated by hyphens. Needs a `Tactic[Uuid.Error]`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      Uuid.parse(t"a0cb16f0-d41e-4c28-862f-bd6164bbcc8c").msb   // -6860364383762101208L
      ```
  entry .extract   reads a UUID from its text, or `Unset` if the text is not one
      As `.parse`, without a tactic: a malformed text gives `Unset`. This is also the
      method behind the companion's `unapply`, so `case Uuid(id) =>` matches valid text.
  entry .decodable   decodes a `Uuid` from text, raising `Uuid.Error` if it is malformed
      Needs a `Tactic[Uuid.Error]`, so `t"…".as[Uuid]` raises through the strategy in scope.
      It is in implicit scope, as are all the companion's givens.
  entry .showable   shows a UUID as its canonical hyphenated text
  entry .inspectable   inspects a UUID as its canonical hyphenated text, the same as `show`
      The canonical form already shows both halves in full and is unmistakable in shape, so
      the debug rendering adds nothing to it.
  entry .communicable   renders a UUID in a message as its canonical text
  entry .encodable   encodes a UUID as its canonical hyphenated text
  entry .encodableInData   encodes a UUID as its sixteen bytes, most significant half first
      Each 64-bit half is written big-endian. Reach it with `uuid.in[Data]`: a plain
      `uuid.encode` would be ambiguous with the `Text` encoding.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      uuid"a0cb16f0-d41e-4c28-862f-bd6164bbcc8c".in[Data].length   // 16
      ```
  entry .Error   raised when text is not a valid UUID
    detail
        Error 349: _`badUuid` is not a valid UUID_. Raised by `Uuid.parse` and by decoding a
        `Uuid` from text. The `uuid"…"` interpolator reports the same message as a compile
        error.
    entry #badUuid   the text which failed to parse
  entry #msb   the most significant 64 bits
  entry #lsb   the least significant 64 bits
  entry #java   the UUID as a `java.util.UUID`, for an API which needs one
  entry #text   the canonical lowercase hyphenated form, such as `a0cb16f0-d41e-4c28-862f-bd6164bbcc8c`
  entry #unary_~   the bitwise inverse of the UUID, written `~uuid`
      Inverts all 128 bits; the result is generally not a valid version 4 identifier, which
      matters only to a consumer that inspects the version bits.
  entry #^   the bitwise exclusive-or of two UUIDs
      Combines both halves bit by bit. Since exclusive-or is its own inverse, `(a ^ b) ^ b
      == a`, which makes it a cheap way to derive a stable identifier from two others and to
      recover either from the result.

entry .uuid   the `uuid"…"` interpolator: a UUID literal, checked as the code compiles
    A macro which parses the literal at compile time and expands to `Uuid(msb, lsb)` with
    the two halves as constants, so no parsing happens at runtime. A malformed literal is a
    compile error: _not-a-uuid is not a valid UUID_. The interpolator takes no substitutions.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    uuid"a0cb16f0-d41e-4c28-862f-bd6164bbcc8c".lsb   // -8777588922722300788L
    ```

entry .jvmInstanceId   a UUID drawn once per running program, identifying this process
    A lazy value, generated with `Uuid()` the first time it is read and the same thereafter
    for the life of the program, so it distinguishes one running instance from another — in
    logs, or in a lock or a message which must name its owner.

entry .internal   the macro behind the `uuid"…"` interpolator
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use.
  entry .uuid   parses a `uuid"…"` literal at compile time
